<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-systems-of-linear-equations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Systems of Linear Equations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Linear algebra begins with a very concrete question: given several linear equations
      in several unknowns, which assignments of values to the unknowns satisfy all of them
      at once? This section fixes the vocabulary and looks at what the answer can look
      like.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-systems-vocabulary">
    <title>Systems, Solutions, and Consistency</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-linear-equation">
      <title>Linear Equation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <term>linear equation</term> in the variables <m>x_1,\ldots,x_n</m> can be written as
          <md>a_1x_1+\cdots+a_nx_n=b,</md>
          where the coefficients <m>a_1,\ldots,a_n</m> and the constant <m>b</m> are known
          real numbers. We seek real values of the variables. A <term>system</term> is a
          collection of equations to be satisfied simultaneously.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-linear-nonlinear">
      <title>Recognizing Linear Equations</title>
      <p>
        The equations <m>2x-3y=5</m> and <m>x=4</m> are linear; a variable may have
        coefficient zero and be absent from an equation. The equations <m>xy=1</m>,
        <m>x^2+y=3</m>, and <m>1/x+y=2</m> are not linear: they contain a product of
        unknowns, a squared unknown, or an unknown in a denominator.
      </p>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-system-vocabulary">
      <title>The Parts of a System</title>
      <p>
        The following is a <term>system</term> of <term>linear equations</term>:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x_1 - 2x_2 - 7x_3 \amp = -1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            -x_1 + 3x_2 + 6x_3 \amp = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>The <term>variables</term> are <m>x_1</m>, <m>x_2</m>, <m>x_3</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The <term>coefficients</term> are the numbers multiplying the variables:
              <m>1, -2, -7</m> in the first equation and <m>-1, 3, 6</m> in the second.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The <term>constant terms</term> are the numbers on the right-hand sides:
              <m>-1</m> and <m>0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="def-solution">
      <title>Solution of a System</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <term>solution to a system</term> of linear equations is an assignment of
          values to the variables that is a solution to <em>every</em> equation in the
          system.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-checking-solutions">
      <title>Checking Candidate Solutions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For the system of <xref ref="eg-system-vocabulary" text="type-global" />, decide
          which of the following are solutions:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \text{(a) } \amp x_1 = -3, \; x_2 = -1, \; x_3 = 0,
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \text{(b) } \amp x_1 = 6, \; x_2 = 0, \; x_3 = 1,
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \text{(c) } \amp x_1 = 0, \; x_2 = 0, \; x_3 = 0.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a) is a solution.</term> Substituting,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              (-3) - 2(-1) - 7(0) \amp = -3 + 2 = -1, \checkmark
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              -(-3) + 3(-1) + 6(0) \amp = 3 - 3 = 0. \checkmark
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b) is also a solution.</term> Substituting,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              6 - 2(0) - 7(1) \amp = 6 - 7 = -1, \checkmark
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              -6 + 3(0) + 6(1) \amp = -6 + 6 = 0. \checkmark
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c) is not a solution.</term> The first equation gives
          <m>0 - 0 - 0 = 0 \neq -1</m>. It fails the first equation, and a solution must
          satisfy every equation in the system.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="def-consistent">
      <title>Consistent and Inconsistent Systems</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A system of linear equations is <term>consistent</term> if it has at least one
          solution, and <term>inconsistent</term> if it has no solutions.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-inconsistent">
      <title>An Inconsistent System</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The system of <xref ref="eg-system-vocabulary" text="type-global" /> is
          consistent, since we exhibited two solutions above. By contrast, consider
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + x_2 + x_3 \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + x_2 + x_3 \amp = -8
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Why is the above system inconsistent?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The two equations have <em>identical</em> left-hand sides. Any assignment of
          values to <m>x_1, x_2, x_3</m> produces one and the same number
          <m>x_1 + x_2 + x_3</m>, and that single number cannot equal both <m>0</m> and
          <m>-8</m>. Hence no assignment satisfies both equations, and the system has no
          solutions.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-graphical-solutions">
    <title>Graphical Solutions</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-two-lines">
      <title>Two Equations in Two Variables</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the system of linear equations in two variables
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x + y \amp = 1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              y - x \amp = 1
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          A solution to this system is a pair of real numbers <m>(x,y)</m> satisfying
          these two equations. Graphically, a solution to this system is the point of
          intersection of these two lines.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Add the equations:
          <md>
            2y = 2, \qquad \text{so} \qquad y = 1.
          </md>
          Substitute <m>y = 1</m> into one of the equations:
          <md>
            x + 1 = 1, \qquad \text{so} \qquad x = 0.
          </md>
          The unique solution is <m>(x,y) = (0,1)</m>, which is exactly the point where
          the two lines cross.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-two-lines-intersect">
      <caption>
        The lines <m>x + y = 1</m> and <m>y - x = 1</m> meet at the single point
        <m>(0,1)</m>, the unique solution of the system.
      </caption>
      <image width="58%">
        <shortdescription>Two lines in the xy-plane, x+y=1 and y-x=1, crossing at the single point (0,1).</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-two-lines-intersect" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(340,340)" margins="10">
            <coordinates bbox="(-3.6,-3.6,3.6,3.6)">
              <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" arrows="2" hticks="(-3,1,3)" vticks="(-3,1,3)"/>
              <line endpoints="((-3.6,4.6),(3.6,-2.6))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
              <line endpoints="((-3.6,-2.6),(3.6,4.6))" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
              <point p="(0,1)" fill="#876801" size="5"/>
              <label p="(0,1)" alignment="e" offset="(10,8)"><m>(0,1)</m></label>
              <label p="(-2.2,3.2)" alignment="e" offset="(6,0)" color="#4A5D23"><m>x+y=1</m></label>
              <label p="(-2.0,-1.0)" alignment="se" offset="(2,-6)"><m>y-x=1</m></label>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      A linear equation in two variables with at least one nonzero coefficient
      describes a line in the <m>xy</m>-coordinate plane. For a system of two such
      equations, there are three possibilities.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-three-possibilities">
      <caption>
        The three possibilities for a system of two linear equations in two variables.
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-no-solution">
          <caption>No solution: the lines are parallel and never meet.</caption>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>Two parallel lines with the same slope that never cross.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure label="pf-no-solution" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(230,230)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="(-2.6,-2.6,2.6,2.6)">
                  <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" arrows="2" decorations="no"/>
                  <line endpoints="((-2.6,-1.6),(2.6,3.6))" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                  <line endpoints="((-2.6,-3.6),(2.6,1.6))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-unique-solution">
          <caption>A unique solution: the lines cross at exactly one point.</caption>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>Two lines with different slopes crossing at one marked point.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure label="pf-unique-solution" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(230,230)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="(-2.6,-2.6,2.6,2.6)">
                  <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" arrows="2" decorations="no"/>
                  <line endpoints="((-2.6,-2.6),(2.6,2.6))" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                  <line endpoints="((-2.6,3.6),(2.6,-1.6))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2"/>
                  <point p="(0.5,0.5)" fill="#876801" size="4"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-infinitely-many">
          <caption>Infinitely many solutions: the two equations describe the same line.</caption>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>A single line drawn twice, a thin black line on top of a thick olive one, so every point is shared.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure label="pf-infinitely-many" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(230,230)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="(-2.6,-2.6,2.6,2.6)">
                  <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" arrows="2" decorations="no"/>
                  <line endpoints="((-2.6,-2.6),(2.6,2.6))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="7"/>
                  <line endpoints="((-2.6,-2.6),(2.6,2.6))" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-coincident-lines">
      <title>A System with Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
      <p>
        The system
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x - y \amp = 0
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2x - 2y \amp = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>
        illustrates the third case. The second equation is just twice the first, so both
        equations describe the same line <m>y = x</m>. Every point on that line is a
        solution, and there are infinitely many of them.
      </p>
    </example>

    <p>
      Notice what is <em>missing</em> from <xref ref="fig-three-possibilities" text="type-global"/>:
      there is no picture of a system with exactly two solutions, or exactly five. Two
      distinct lines can only meet once. That is no accident of the plane; it holds for
      any number of equations in any number of variables, and the reason is a short
      calculation.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-zero-one-infinitely-many">
      <title>Zero, One, or Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A system of linear equations has either no solution, exactly one solution, or
          infinitely many solutions.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          We show that a system with <em>two different</em> solutions must have infinitely
          many. Suppose <m>(u_1, \ldots, u_n)</m> and <m>(v_1, \ldots, v_n)</m> are two
          different solutions of a system in the variables <m>x_1, \ldots, x_n</m>. For any
          real number <m>t</m>, consider the assignment
          <md>
            x_1 = u_1 + t(v_1 - u_1), \quad x_2 = u_2 + t(v_2 - u_2), \quad \ldots, \quad
            x_n = u_n + t(v_n - u_n).
          </md>
          Take any equation <m>a_1 x_1 + \cdots + a_n x_n = b</m> of the system. Both
          given solutions satisfy it, so <m>a_1 u_1 + \cdots + a_n u_n = b</m> and
          <m>a_1 v_1 + \cdots + a_n v_n = b</m>. Substituting the new assignment,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i \bigl(u_i + t(v_i - u_i)\bigr)
              \amp = \sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i u_i + t\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i v_i - \sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i u_i\Bigr)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = b + t(b - b) = b.
            </mrow>
          </md>
          So the new assignment satisfies every equation of the system, for every real
          <m>t</m>. Finally, different values of <m>t</m> give different assignments:
          the two given solutions differ in some coordinate, say <m>u_k \neq v_k</m>, and
          then <m>u_k + t(v_k - u_k)</m> takes a different value for each <m>t</m>. Hence
          there are infinitely many solutions.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      Geometrically, the assignments in the proof trace out the entire line through the
      two given solutions, exactly as in the third picture of
      <xref ref="fig-three-possibilities" text="type-global"/>. We will meet this
      <q>line through two solutions</q> again, in vector language, in
      <xref ref="sec-homogeneous-systems" text="type-global"/>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-parametric-form">
    <title>Parametric Form of a Solution Set</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-parametric-solution">
      <title>Describing Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The system of linear equations in three variables that we saw earlier,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 - 2x_2 - 7x_3 \amp = -1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              -x_1 + 3x_2 + 6x_3 \amp = 0,
            </mrow>
          </md>
          has solutions <m>x_1 = -3 + 9s</m>, <m>x_2 = -1 + s</m>, <m>x_3 = s</m>, where
          <m>s</m> is any real number (<m>s \in \R</m>).
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          To obtain all the solutions, add the two original equations to get
          <m>x_2-x_3=-1</m>. Thus <m>x_2=-1+x_3</m>. Substitution in the first equation gives
          <md>x_1=-1+2(-1+x_3)+7x_3=-3+9x_3.</md>
          Every solution must therefore have the stated form with <m>s=x_3</m>.
          Conversely, substituting these expressions in the original equations gives
          <md>
            <mrow>(-3+9s)-2(-1+s)-7s \amp =-1,</mrow>
            <mrow>-(-3+9s)+3(-1+s)+6s \amp =0</mrow>
          </md>
          for every real <m>s</m>. Hence every parameter value works, and the formulas
          describe exactly all the solutions.
        </p>
        <p>
          The number <m>s</m> is called a <term>parameter</term>, and
          <md>
            x_1 = -3 + 9s, \quad x_2 = -1 + s, \quad x_3 = s, \quad \text{where } s \in \R
          </md>
          is called the <term>solution set</term> in <term>parametric form</term>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Notice that the two solutions we found in
          <xref ref="eg-checking-solutions" text="type-global" /> are recovered by taking
          <m>s = 0</m> and <m>s = 1</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              s = 0 \amp \;\longrightarrow\; (x_1, x_2, x_3) = (-3, -1, 0),
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              s = 1 \amp \;\longrightarrow\; (x_1, x_2, x_3) = (6, 0, 1).
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-geometric-interpretation">
      <title>Geometric Interpretation of the Solution Set</title>
      <p>
        What does the solution set of <xref ref="eg-parametric-solution" text="type-global"/>
        look like? Each of the two equations describes a plane in three-dimensional
        coordinate space, and these two planes intersect in a line: the line traced out
        by the parametric equations above as <m>s</m> runs through <m>\R</m>. (Two planes
        can also be parallel and distinct, giving no solutions, or coincide, giving a
        whole plane of solutions; these particular planes meet in a line.)
      </p>
    </remark>

    <p>
      The explorer uses the original first equation and <m>x_1-9x_2=6</m> to show
      the same solution line at a wider angle. To check this directly, the first
      equation gives <m>x_1=-1+2x_2+7x_3</m>. Substitution into either the original
      second equation or the new second equation gives <m>x_2-x_3=-1</m>.
      Thus the two pairs of equations have exactly the same solutions.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Predict before exploring.</em> As <m>s</m> changes, can the point
      <m>(-3+9s,-1+s,s)</m> leave either of these two planes? Suppose a third equation
      is added to the system. Which of the three candidates
      <md>
        x_3 = 0, \qquad x_2 - x_3 = -1, \qquad x_2 - x_3 = 0
      </md>
      will keep the entire line of solutions, which will cut it down to a single point,
      and which will leave no solutions at all?
    </p>
    <p xml:id="intro-solution-set-explorer">
      <em>Explore the solution set.</em>
      The moving point lies on the intersection of
      <m>x_1-2x_2-7x_3=-1</m> and <m>x_1-9x_2=6</m>, which describe the same
      solution line as <xref ref="eg-parametric-solution" text="type-global"/>. The wine highlight
      always represents the common solutions of <em>all</em> active equations.
      Use the parameter slider, third-plane menu, and camera controls, or play
      the captioned guide. The window shows only part of the infinite planes
      and line.
    </p>
    <interactive xml:id="int-solution-set-explorer" iframe="demos-manim/solution-set.html" width="100%" aspect="4:5">
      <shortdescription>Two planes intersect in the line x1=-3+9s, x2=-1+s, x3=s. A third plane leaves one point, the whole line, or no common solution.</shortdescription>
      <static>
        <image source="images/solution-set-static.svg" width="95%">
          <shortdescription>The planes x1-2x2-7x3=-1 and x1-9x2=6 share the wine solution line x1=-3+9s, x2=-1+s, x3=s.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
    <p>
      Along this line, substitution gives
      <md>
        <mrow>(-3+9s)-2(-1+s)-7s \amp = -1,</mrow>
        <mrow>(-3+9s)-9(-1+s) \amp = 6.</mrow>
      </md>
      Adding <m>x_3=0</m> forces <m>s=0</m>, leaving just <m>(-3,-1,0)</m>.
      Adding <m>x_2-x_3=-1</m> keeps the whole line because
      <m>(-1+s)-s=-1</m> for every <m>s</m>. Adding <m>x_2-x_3=0</m>
      leaves no common solution because it would require <m>-1=0</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Why does one free parameter describe a line? What changes when the third equation is added?</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      So far we have found solution sets by ad hoc substitution. The rest of the chapter
      develops a systematic method for the central computational problem.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-central-problem">
      <title>The Central Problem</title>
      <p>
        Find all solutions to a system of <m>m</m> linear equations in <m>n</m> variables,
        i.e., <em>solve a system of linear equations</em>.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-ch1-systems">
    <title>Practice: Equations and Solutions</title>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-linearity">
      <statement><p>Which equations are linear in <m>x,y</m>? Explain:
      (a) <m>3x-2y=7</m>; (b) <m>xy=4</m>; (c) <m>x^2+y=1</m>;
      (d) <m>y=5</m>; (e) <m>x/2+y=0</m>.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>(a), (d), and (e) are linear. In (b) the unknowns are multiplied;
      in (c) an unknown is squared.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-all-equations">
      <statement><p>For <m>x+y=3</m>, <m>x-y=1</m>, test the pairs
      <m>(2,1)</m> and <m>(3,0)</m>. Why is satisfying just one equation insufficient?</p></statement>
      <answer><p>Only <m>(2,1)</m> is a solution. The pair <m>(3,0)</m> satisfies the
      first equation but not the second. A solution must satisfy every equation.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-parametric-check">
      <statement><p>Describe all solutions of <m>x+2y=5</m> using a parameter.
      Show both that every solution has your form and that every parameter value works.</p></statement>
      <answer><p><m>x=5-2s</m>, <m>y=s</m>, for any real <m>s</m>.
      Every solution has this form by taking <m>s=y</m>; conversely,
      <m>(5-2s)+2s=5</m> for every real <m>s</m>.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
